\section{Standard errors, degrees of freedom and intervals}{

Inference is Wald t at the degrees of freedom survey writes on the fit:
\code{df.residual} for \code{svyglm} and \code{svyolr}, \code{degf.resid} or
\code{degf.residual} for the two Cox engines. This is what
\code{\link[survey:regTermTest]{survey::regTermTest()}} takes as its denominator. It is not
\code{survey::degf(design)}, and it is not re-derived here: the engines of
survey do not share one expression (a Cox fit carries
\code{degf(design) - p + 1} although it has no intercept for the \code{+ 1} to
cancel, so it ends one above the two other classes). The value is read
off the object, and the note prints it. The interval uses the same
distribution, and so do the average marginal effects.
}

\section{Fit statistics}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"aic"}: \code{svyglm} keeps an \code{AIC} row. survey's
\code{extractAIC.svyglm} computes the design-based AIC of Lumley & Scott
(2015).
\item \code{"eff_p"}: the effective number of parameters of the design,
the first element of survey's \code{extractAIC} (the sum of the
Rao-Scott eigenvalues), reported beside the AIC on request.
\item \code{"concordance"}: the concordance of a \code{svycoxph} fit goes to the
note, or to a row on request.
}
}

\section{What is refused, and why}{

\itemize{
\item Every model-derived variance (\verb{HC*}, \verb{CR*}, \code{"bootstrap"},
\code{"jackknife"}). The design is the variance authority: the fit's
Taylor or replicate variance is already the robust variance for the
declared design. The way to change the estimator is to change the
design.
\item \code{"bic"}, \code{"deviance"} and \code{"log_lik"} for a table of design
fits, and every likelihood statistic (AIC, BIC, log-likelihood,
deviance, pseudo-R-squared) for \code{svyolr} and \code{svycoxph}. A
design-based fit is estimated without a likelihood, and survey's own
\code{deviance()} returns a sign-flipped likelihood-ratio statistic on
one Cox engine and a bare zero on the other. \code{BIC.svyglm} requires a
maximal model and has no default.
\item \code{nested = TRUE}. There is no likelihood to compare, so every
change statistic would be empty, and a block of empty rows reads
like an answer. Put the models side by side with \code{nested = FALSE},
and test a term under the design with \code{\link[survey:regTermTest]{survey::regTermTest()}}.
\item The AME for \code{svycoxph}, on the same ground as for a Cox model
without a design (\link{table_regression_survival}).
\item The \code{"rmst"} and \code{"risk_diff"} columns for \code{svycoxph}: their
uncertainty comes from resampling subjects, which ignores the strata
and clusters the design declares. Use \code{\link[survey:svykm]{survey::svykm()}} for a
marginal curve.
\item \code{standardized}.
}
}

